<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elephanta Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th–6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Elephanta Caves are rock-cut cave temples on Elephanta Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> harbour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, mostly dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and carved in the 5th to 6th centuries. The main cave holds a huge three-headed image of Shiva, the Sadashiva. Their patrons are uncertain, and some historians attribute them to the Kalachuris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a>. They became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves.jpg" alt="Elephanta Caves" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Haugen · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephanta_Caves_Trimurti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancient history of Elephanta Island is unknown in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist records. Archaeological finds suggest human settlement by possibly the 2nd century BCE, when Buddhists seem to have been the first occupants and raised a large stupa with seven smaller ones around it. Coins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a> of the 4th century CE have been found on the island.</p><p>No surviving record of the Gupta era mentions the caves, so the century of their making has been disputed, with dates ranging from the late 5th to the late 8th century. Colonial-era historians attributed them to the Rashtrakutas in the 7th century or later, a theory later findings have discredited.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> place the temples in the 5th and 6th centuries. Contemporary scholars generally put their completion in the second quarter of the 6th century, as part of the artistic flowering of the Gupta era, and attribute them to King Krishnaraja of the Kalachuri dynasty, on numismatic evidence, inscriptions, construction style and the dating of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> cave temples such as Ajanta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The island, about 10 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, has five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> caves on its western hill and two Buddhist caves and stupa mounds on its eastern one, all hewn from solid basalt. Cave 1, the Great Cave, is 39.63 metres square in plan, with a hall of pillars and several entrances. The carvings, mostly in high relief, narrate Hindu legends and show Hindu and Buddhist ideas together. The best known are the three-faced Sadashiva, 5.45 metres high, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Nataraja</a>, lord of the dance, and Yogishvara, lord of yogis. The main temple and the other caves are laid out in a mandala pattern. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> also appear in the art.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Portuguese named the island Elefante, which became Elephanta, after elephant statues they found there. The Great Cave had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> place of worship until they arrived, when the island ceased to be an active one, and Cave 6 was used as a Christian church at some point between 1534 and 1682. Much of the sculpture has been defaced or damaged. James Burgess's publication of the site's condition in 1871 brought wider attention, British Indian officials began protecting it in 1909, and the Indian government restored the monuments in the 1970s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> now maintains them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elephanta Caves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some historians attribute the great Shiva cave at <b>Elephanta</b> and early caves at Ellora to their patronage, though this is debated.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalachuris-of-mahishmati">Kalachuris of Mahishmati</a></li></ul></section>
